What is MongoDB?
===================
--> MongoDB is a NoSQL database used to store data in a flexible, JSON-like format instead of rows and columns.

--> In simple words: 
Traditional databases (like MySQL) store data in tables
MongoDB stores data in documents
These documents look very similar to JavaScript objects, so it’s easy for developers—especially when working with Node.js.

--> Documents are grouped into collections. A collection is similar to a table, but no fixed structure is required

Example:
{
  name: "Phone",
  price: 15000,
  stock: 10
}


fixed structure means 
1️⃣ MySQL (FIXED STRUCTURE)

First, you must create a table: You CANNOT add new fields without changing the table

If later you want to store color for only one product ❌

INSERT INTO products (name, price, color)
VALUES ('Laptop', 50000, 'Black'); ❌ ERROR


👉 Because color column does not exist

To fix this, you must do:

ALTER TABLE products ADD color VARCHAR(20);

That’s why MySQL is called fixed structure.

2️⃣ MongoDB (NO FIXED STRUCTURE)

Now the same example in MongoDB.

Same collection: products

First document:
db.products.insertOne({
  name: "Phone",
  price: 15000
});


Second document (extra field added):
db.products.insertOne({
  name: "Laptop",
  price: 50000,
  color: "Black"
});

👉 Both are stored without any error
👉 No table change
👉 No schema update

🔑 Final simple meaning

MySQL → “All rows must look the same”

MongoDB → “Each document can look different